{"id":"mole-concept","title":"Chemistry: the mole concept","subject":"Chemistry","label":"JAMB · WAEC","exams":["jamb","waec"],"language":"en","seconds":165,"duration":164.54,"audio":"voice.mp3","words":441,"sections":[{"id":"hook","title":"A dozen puzzle","start":0,"end":13.385},{"id":"explain","title":"Mole basics","start":13.385,"end":46.755},{"id":"explain2","title":"Particles and gases","start":46.755,"end":63.685},{"id":"example","title":"Water example","start":63.685,"end":89.235},{"id":"check","title":"Quick check","start":89.235,"end":98.285},{"id":"answer","title":"Answer","start":98.285,"end":107.505},{"id":"mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","start":107.505,"end":136.385},{"id":"recap","title":"Recap and tip","start":136.385,"end":164.54}],"lines":[{"s":0,"a":0.5,"b":6.13,"text":"I'm Kora. Here's a small puzzle. A dozen eggs means twelve eggs, no matter the market.","w":[0.54,0.7,1.7,1.98,2.06,2.34,3.08,3.2,3.58,3.86,4.18,4.58,5.22,5.36,5.62,5.72]},{"s":0,"a":6.43,"b":12.96,"text":"So what do chemists mean when they say one mole of water? It sounds strange, but it is just a counting word for particles.","w":[6.52,6.7,6.88,7.06,7.5,7.78,7.92,8.08,8.4,8.58,8.82,8.92,9.88,10.02,10.38,10.84,11,11.1,11.24,11.5,11.58,11.98,12.2,12.36]},{"s":1,"a":13.81,"b":19.71,"text":"One mole is an amount of substance containing six point zero two times ten to the power twenty-three particles.","w":[13.81,14.02,14.28,14.44,14.6,14.97,15.13,15.65,16.23,16.44,16.76,17.02,17.23,17.55,17.76,17.92,18.13,18.45,19.13]},{"s":1,"a":20.01,"b":25.28,"text":"That number is the Avogadro constant. Think of it as chemistry's dozen, only far larger.","w":[20.04,20.32,20.74,20.86,21.08,21.76,22.72,22.98,23.08,23.22,23.34,23.88,24.34,24.62,24.88]},{"s":1,"a":25.58,"b":32.14,"text":"Molar mass is the mass of one mole in grams per mole. You find it by adding relative atomic masses.","w":[25.68,26.04,26.42,26.54,26.68,27,27.22,27.38,27.74,27.88,28.32,28.5,29.4,29.6,29.88,29.98,30.26,30.6,31.1,31.58]},{"s":1,"a":32.44,"b":39.49,"text":"Remember hydrogen is one, carbon is twelve, oxygen is sixteen, sodium is twenty-three, and calcium is forty.","w":[32.44,33.02,33.6,33.8,34.12,34.57,34.77,35.29,35.74,35.93,36.51,36.97,37.16,38.07,38.33,38.84,39.04]},{"s":1,"a":39.79,"b":46.33,"text":"The key formula is moles equals mass divided by molar mass. Write the units before you put in numbers.","w":[39.82,39.94,40.22,40.74,40.92,41.5,41.88,42.38,42.8,42.98,43.32,44.3,44.52,44.72,45.04,45.38,45.5,45.68,45.8]},{"s":2,"a":47.18,"b":53.13,"text":"For particles, moles times six point zero two times ten to the power twenty-three gives the number of particles.","w":[47.18,47.39,47.97,48.29,48.6,48.81,49.13,49.39,49.6,49.92,50.13,50.29,50.5,50.81,51.5,51.81,52.02,52.39,52.55]},{"s":2,"a":53.43,"b":60.04,"text":"For gases at standard temperature and pressure, one mole occupies twenty-two point four cubic decimetres.","w":[53.43,53.68,54.05,54.24,54.8,55.55,55.8,56.42,56.67,56.98,57.55,58.23,58.61,58.92,59.29]},{"s":2,"a":60.34,"b":63.26,"text":"That is the same as twenty-two point four litres.","w":[60.34,60.63,60.81,61.04,61.33,61.51,62.15,62.5,62.79]},{"s":3,"a":64.11,"b":67.82,"text":"Let's use water. How many moles are in thirty-six grams of water?","w":[64.11,64.45,64.67,65.07,65.29,65.57,65.91,66.13,66.3,66.92,67.26,67.43]},{"s":3,"a":68.27,"b":73.93,"text":"First, find the molar mass of H two O. Two plus sixteen equals eighteen grams per mole.","w":[68.3,68.98,69.24,69.36,69.72,70.04,70.32,70.44,70.68,70.82,71.42,71.68,72.36,72.74,73.12,73.5,73.66]},{"s":3,"a":74.23,"b":78.72,"text":"Then moles equals thirty-six divided by eighteen, which is two moles.","w":[74.23,74.55,74.94,75.38,76.09,76.6,76.8,77.44,77.82,78.01,78.27]},{"s":3,"a":79.02,"b":83.95,"text":"For molecules, multiply two by six point zero two times ten to the power twenty-three.","w":[79.02,79.25,79.87,80.38,80.61,80.78,81,81.34,81.63,81.85,82.19,82.42,82.59,82.82,83.16]},{"s":3,"a":84.25,"b":88.81,"text":"That gives one point two zero four times ten to the power twenty-four molecules.","w":[84.25,84.53,84.87,85.09,85.43,85.66,85.94,86.22,86.56,86.78,86.95,87.18,87.52,88.19]},{"s":4,"a":89.66,"b":95.25,"text":"Now try this. How many moles are in eighty grams of sodium hydroxide, N A O H?","w":[89.72,89.9,90.14,90.88,91.06,91.32,91.66,91.78,91.98,92.26,92.64,92.78,93.16,94.38,94.48,94.7,95.06]},{"s":4,"a":95.7,"b":97.86,"text":"Pause here and try it. I'll wait.","w":[95.72,96.04,96.26,96.38,96.7,97.36,97.54]},{"s":5,"a":98.71,"b":100.28,"text":"The answer is two moles.","w":[98.72,98.92,99.32,99.52,99.78]},{"s":5,"a":100.58,"b":107.08,"text":"The molar mass is twenty-three plus sixteen plus one, which equals forty, so eighty divided by forty gives two.","w":[100.58,100.81,101.16,101.45,101.62,102.38,102.67,103.13,103.42,103.71,104.06,104.47,104.87,105.05,105.45,105.92,106.09,106.44,106.79]},{"s":6,"a":107.93,"b":116.41,"text":"One common mistake is dividing molar mass by mass. Keep the formula the right way up: moles equals mass divided by molar mass.","w":[108.1,108.32,108.7,109.2,109.32,109.82,110.16,110.5,110.74,111.68,111.9,112,112.46,112.6,112.8,113.04,113.64,114.12,114.5,114.9,115.38,115.6,115.94]},{"s":6,"a":116.71,"b":123.97,"text":"Another is forgetting the subscript in H two O. The two doubles hydrogen, so the molar mass is eighteen, not seventeen.","w":[116.78,117.18,117.3,117.74,117.88,118.5,118.78,118.92,119.26,119.68,119.82,120.06,120.4,121.36,121.52,121.64,121.96,122.32,122.48,123.24,123.44]},{"s":6,"a":124.27,"b":127.79,"text":"A third mistake is mixing up atoms and molecules.","w":[124.36,124.52,124.86,125.58,125.78,126.16,126.5,126.96,127.1]},{"s":6,"a":128.09,"b":135.96,"text":"Water has three atoms in each molecule, but one mole of water means six point zero two times ten to the power twenty-three molecules.","w":[128.09,128.44,128.68,129.03,129.38,129.56,129.85,130.44,130.67,130.91,131.2,131.38,131.73,132.08,132.32,132.67,132.96,133.2,133.55,133.79,133.96,134.2,134.55,135.31]},{"s":7,"a":136.81,"b":143.2,"text":"Recap. A mole is a counting amount: six point zero two times ten to the power twenty-three particles.","w":[136.81,137.25,137.37,137.69,137.88,138,138.56,139.07,139.32,139.69,140,140.26,140.63,140.88,141.07,141.32,141.7,142.51]},{"s":7,"a":143.5,"b":149.48,"text":"Molar mass is found by adding relative atomic masses, and moles equal mass divided by molar mass.","w":[143.62,144.04,144.38,144.52,144.84,145.08,145.4,145.82,146.2,147.02,147.2,147.64,147.94,148.2,148.58,148.76,149.04]},{"s":7,"a":149.78,"b":155.17,"text":"For gases at standard temperature and pressure, one mole takes twenty-two point four cubic decimetres.","w":[149.78,149.99,150.3,150.46,150.93,151.56,151.77,152.29,152.5,152.76,153.08,153.65,153.97,154.23,154.54]},{"s":7,"a":155.47,"b":159.88,"text":"Exam tip: write the formula triangle and the units before putting in numbers.","w":[155.56,155.96,156.68,156.92,157.06,157.5,158.06,158.16,158.34,158.66,158.98,159.28,159.4]},{"s":7,"a":160.18,"b":163.94,"text":"Keep practising with Knowzone, and you will handle mole questions calmly.","w":[160.18,160.42,160.9,161.04,161.92,162.04,162.16,162.34,162.72,163.02,163.54]}],"scenes":[{"at":0.25,"heading":"A dozen puzzle","items":[{"at":0.4,"kind":"text","text":"A dozen = 12, always"},{"at":6.33,"kind":"text","text":"1 mole = counting word for particles"}]},{"at":13.56,"heading":"Mole basics","items":[{"at":13.71,"kind":"formula","text":"1 mol = 6.02 × 10²³ particles"},{"at":19.91,"kind":"term","text":"Avogadro constant","detail":"6.02 × 10²³ particles per mole; chemistry's dozen"},{"at":25.48,"kind":"term","text":"Molar mass","detail":"Mass of 1 mole in g/mol; add relative atomic masses"},{"at":32.34,"kind":"text","text":"H=1, C=12, O=16, Na=23, Ca=40"},{"at":39.69,"kind":"formula","text":"n = m ÷ M; units first"}]},{"at":46.93,"heading":"Particles and gases","items":[{"at":47.08,"kind":"formula","text":"N = n × 6.02 × 10²³"},{"at":53.33,"kind":"formula","text":"1 mol gas at STP = 22.4 dm³"},{"at":60.24,"kind":"text","text":"22.4 dm³ = 22.4 L"}]},{"at":63.86,"heading":"Water example","items":[{"at":64.01,"kind":"text","text":"How many moles in 36 g water?"},{"at":68.17,"kind":"step","text":"M(H2O) = 2 + 16 = 18 g/mol"},{"at":74.13,"kind":"step","text":"n = 36 ÷ 18 = 2 mol"},{"at":78.92,"kind":"step","text":"N = 2 × 6.02 × 10²³"},{"at":84.15,"kind":"step","text":"= 1.204 × 10²⁴ molecules"}]},{"at":89.23,"heading":"Quick check","items":[{"at":89.23,"kind":"question","text":"How many moles are in 80 g of sodium hydroxide, NaOH?"}]},{"at":98.46,"heading":"Answer","items":[{"at":98.61,"kind":"text","text":"Answer: 2 moles"},{"at":100.48,"kind":"step","text":"M(NaOH)=23+16+1=40; 80÷40=2 mol"}]},{"at":107.68,"heading":"Common mistakes","items":[{"at":107.83,"kind":"wrong","text":"Divide molar mass by mass"},{"at":116.61,"kind":"wrong","text":"Ignore subscript in H2O"},{"at":124.17,"kind":"wrong","text":"Mix up atoms and molecules"},{"at":127.99,"kind":"text","text":"H2O has 3 atoms per molecule"}]},{"at":136.56,"heading":"Recap and tip","items":[{"at":136.71,"kind":"text","text":"Mole: counting amount = 6.02 × 10²³ particles"},{"at":143.4,"kind":"text","text":"M = sum of Ar; n = m ÷ M"},{"at":149.68,"kind":"text","text":"1 mol gas at STP = 22.4 dm³"},{"at":155.37,"kind":"tip","text":"Write formula triangle and units before numbers"},{"at":160.08,"kind":"text","text":"Practise with Knowzone"}]}],"check":{"at":98.11,"seconds":10,"question":"How many moles are in 80 g of sodium hydroxide, NaOH?","answer":"2 moles; M(NaOH) = 23 + 16 + 1 = 40 g/mol, so 80 ÷ 40 = 2 mol."},"fps":30,"mouth":"","level":"","credits":[],"voice":"qwen3-tts-12hz-1.7b (cloned voice)"}